window.SUPER_BANK.push(...[{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-143","chapter":6,"mode":"scenarios","topic":"Wound-care synthesis","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A client declines wound care because it hurt previously. The HCA fears complications and wants to warn that sepsis is inevitable. Which response best combines communication and professional development?","choices":["Avoid discussing the refusal until the HCA has completed advanced wound-care education.","Independently diagnose infection to establish urgency and perform the intervention despite the stated refusal.","Use the strongest possible complication warning to secure agreement, then seek education afterward.","Acknowledge pain, regulate personal fear, report the refusal and observations appropriately, document facts, and seek approved education or supervision about the knowledge gap."],"answer":[3],"explain":"Pain is a barrier to explore and report. Possible complications should not be presented as inevitable, and an HCA’s fear does not supply authority to diagnose or override. Learning and supervision can improve future communication within role.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-143","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-144","chapter":6,"mode":"scenarios","topic":"Home safety synthesis","difficulty":2,"prompt":"During a home visit, an HCA keeps keys/phone accessible, uses a neutral tone, and safely withdraws under procedure when risk escalates. A student calls these unconscious defence mechanisms. Which correction and follow-up are strongest?","choices":["The neutral tone proves repression; keep practising it without reporting because emotional control resolves the safety concern.","Every action that reduces anxiety is an unconscious mechanism, so no further safety analysis is needed.","These may be conscious safety strategies; later examine warning signs, context and personal reactions, report the situation, and identify appropriate preparation or plan review.","The withdrawal is displacement; the HCA should have remained until the task was finished to avoid reinforcing it."],"answer":[2],"explain":"A deliberate safety action is not automatically an unconscious defence mechanism. Necessary safety responses come first, followed by appropriate reporting, support and reflection. Emotional regulation does not replace safety procedures.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-144","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-145","chapter":6,"mode":"scenarios","topic":"Advocacy synthesis","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA observes a recurring change that a visiting clinician has not seen. An initial report receives little response. Which next step best supports advocacy within role?","choices":["Present specific observations, timing and relevant client statements, relate them to the plan, and use the appropriate escalation/documentation route to request review.","Make a diagnosis before reporting again so the concern will sound credible.","Change the care plan to demonstrate the importance of the observation.","Conclude that the observation is irrelevant because the clinician did not witness it."],"answer":[0],"explain":"HCAs may contribute direct observations unavailable to visiting clinicians. Clear facts and appropriate escalation support advocacy without independent diagnosis or unilateral treatment changes.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-145","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-146","chapter":6,"mode":"scenarios","topic":"Orders synthesis","difficulty":2,"prompt":"A client expresses a new treatment preference inconsistent with the current authorized order. The family asks the HCA to change it in the chart. What is the best response?","choices":["Ask the family to choose the order and record their decision as the HCA’s authorization.","Revise the order because a reported preference automatically supplies authority to any worker who heard it.","Ignore the statement because an existing order makes changing preferences irrelevant.","Acknowledge and report the expressed preference for authorized review, explain the HCA cannot independently revise the order, and follow the applicable orders, plan and emergency procedures."],"answer":[3],"explain":"Preferences matter and should be communicated for appropriate review. The HCA cannot independently change the order. The scenario does not provide enough information or authority to decide treatment; applicable procedures remain important.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-146","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-147","chapter":6,"mode":"scenarios","topic":"End-of-life synthesis","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA finds an unresponsive client and is unsure whether the situation was expected. A coworker proposes pronouncing death and assuming an autopsy is mandatory. Which response is most appropriate?","choices":["Pronounce death first so the reporting process has a definitive starting point.","Assume that uncertainty about the plan allows the HCA to choose any response that feels reasonable.","Wait to determine whether an autopsy is required before using the emergency procedure.","Follow applicable emergency/reporting procedures and authorized directions promptly, communicate observations, and avoid independent pronouncement or assumptions about universal autopsy requirements."],"answer":[3],"explain":"The brief emphasizes prompt, role-appropriate procedures and reporting. HCAs do not independently pronounce death, and unexpected circumstances do not establish a universal autopsy or coroner-attendance requirement.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-147","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-148","chapter":6,"mode":"scenarios","topic":"Documentation synthesis","difficulty":2,"prompt":"The only known facts are that a client turned away and said, “Not now.” An HCA drafts, “Client was stubborn and wanted attention. I followed the plan, so I am legally protected.” Which revision is best supported?","choices":["Replace “stubborn” with “noncompliant,” retain the attention-seeking explanation, and add that the nurse was notified even if this is unknown.","Record the observed turning away and quote, add relevant actions/notifications only if actually known or performed, and remove the motive labels and immunity claim.","Keep the motive labels in quotation marks so they become objective facts.","Delete the client’s words and record only the HCA’s confidence that the plan was followed."],"answer":[1],"explain":"Objective documentation separates observed actions and attributed words from inferred motives. Unknown actions must not be invented. Following the plan and documenting accurately do not guarantee immunity from liability.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-148","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-149","chapter":6,"mode":"scenarios","topic":"Mature responses synthesis","difficulty":2,"prompt":"While supporting a grieving family, one worker knowingly postpones personal distress and later processes it; another insists the death did not occur; a third jokes insensitively to silence grief. Which interpretation is most accurate?","choices":["The first illustrates denial, the second suppression, and the third is appropriate if the worker intended to help.","The first illustrates suppression, the second denial, and the third is not justified merely by calling humour mature.","The first illustrates sublimation, the second projection, and the third proves emotional resilience.","The first illustrates repression, the second anticipation, and the third is mature whenever it reduces discussion."],"answer":[1],"explain":"Temporary postponement of recognized distress is suppression; refusal to accept reality is denial. Mature humour acknowledges seriousness and fits the situation. Intention or a defence label does not justify silencing or belittling grief.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-149","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-6/data.js:ch6-150","chapter":6,"mode":"scenarios","topic":"Reflection and reflexivity synthesis","difficulty":2,"prompt":"An HCA reflects, “I wanted to reassure the client. My authority may have made disagreement difficult. I assumed silence meant agreement.” Which follow-up most fully integrates reflective and reflexive practice?","choices":["Assume authority definitely caused the silence, then avoid any further clarification to prevent pressure.","Focus only on the client’s communication style because professional authority cannot influence willingness to disagree.","Treat the reassuring intention as proof of benefit and use silence as the outcome measure.","Review the experience, examine how personal assumptions and position may have influenced it, invite preferences/check understanding in later encounters, and evaluate the responses."],"answer":[3],"explain":"Reflection reviews experience to guide later practice; reflexivity examines the worker’s assumptions, position and influence, including during interaction. Silence and good intentions do not establish agreement or benefit. The proposed influence should be explored rather than treated as proven.","source":"Supplied Chapter 6, class slides and instructor source brief; see source notes for coverage limits.","references":[{"href":"../chapter-6/source-notes.md","title":"Source notes and study guide"}],"written":false,"originalId":"ch6-150","group":"Chapter 6","bank":"Chapter 6","sourceFiles":["chapter-6/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_4","chapter":13,"mode":"level1","topic":"Résumés & portfolios","prompt":"An employer requests a document that expands a résumé with academic and professional achievements such as teaching, research and awards. Which document fits?","choices":["A curriculum vitae (CV)","A chronological résumé limited to employment history","A cover letter explaining interest in one vacancy","A reference list documenting others’ opinions of the applicant"],"answer":[0],"explain":"A CV may expand on résumé information with achievements such as research, teaching and awards. A letter of application and a reference list serve different purposes.","source":"Chapter 13 — Resumé; Day 4 presentation, slide 4","difficulty":1,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Resumé","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_4"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 4","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_4"}],"originalId":"c13_4","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_7","chapter":13,"mode":"level1","topic":"Job searches & applications","prompt":"Which letter responds to an advertised opening?","choices":["Solicited letter of application","Unsolicited letter of application","Thank-you note","Reference letter"],"answer":[0],"explain":"A solicited letter responds to an advertised vacancy. An unsolicited letter asks about possible openings without responding to an advertisement.","source":"Chapter 13 — Preparing a Letter of Application; Day 4 presentation, slide 15","difficulty":1,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Preparing a Letter of Application","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_7"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 15","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_7"}],"originalId":"c13_7","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_8","chapter":13,"mode":"level1","topic":"Job searches & applications","prompt":"You ask an agency whether it has any openings, without seeing an advertisement. Your letter is:","choices":["Unsolicited","Solicited","A CV","A conditional offer"],"answer":[0],"explain":"An unsolicited letter inquires about potential openings. A solicited letter responds to an advertised position; a CV summarizes qualifications and achievements.","source":"Chapter 13 — Preparing a Letter of Application; Day 4 presentation, slide 15","difficulty":1,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Preparing a Letter of Application","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_8"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 15","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_8"}],"originalId":"c13_8","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_10","chapter":13,"mode":"level1","topic":"Job searches & applications","prompt":"Which person is generally a strong reference?","choices":["A previous employer who knows your work","A friend who has never worked with you","A family member","Someone whose permission you have not requested"],"answer":[0],"explain":"A former employer who knows your work can describe observed skills and conduct. Choose someone who has agreed to help; a personal relationship alone is not a professional reference.","source":"Chapter 13 — Selecting References; Day 4 presentation, slide 13","difficulty":1,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Selecting References","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_10"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 13","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_10"}],"originalId":"c13_10","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_15","chapter":13,"mode":"level1","topic":"Interviews & employment offers","prompt":"A job offer is conditional. What does this mean?","choices":["Specified requirements must be met before the employment can proceed as offered.","The employer has finalized every hiring requirement but may change the shift.","The applicant can complete requirements after starting, regardless of the offer’s wording.","Accepting the offer automatically satisfies any outstanding checks."],"answer":[0],"explain":"A conditional offer depends on meeting stated requirements. Examples include health documentation or a record check; receiving the offer does not mean those conditions have been satisfied.","source":"Chapter 13 — Conditional Offer; Day 4 presentation, slide 29","difficulty":1,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Conditional Offer","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_15"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 29","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_15"}],"originalId":"c13_15","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_16","chapter":13,"mode":"level1","topic":"Interviews & employment offers","prompt":"Before accepting a contract position, what should you clarify?","choices":["Contract length, terms and what is included","The title and pay, assuming the length follows the normal probation period","The start date, leaving responsibilities to be clarified after signing","The hourly rate, assuming employee benefits apply to every contract"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Read the contract’s duration, terms, inclusions and exclusions before agreeing. The title or starting time alone does not establish your obligations.","source":"Chapter 13 — Accepting an Offer; Day 4 presentation, slide 29","difficulty":1,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Accepting an Offer","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_16"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 29","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_16"}],"originalId":"c13_16","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_17","chapter":13,"mode":"level1","topic":"Interviews & employment offers","prompt":"What should you learn about benefits?","choices":["Eligibility, coverage and when benefits begin","The list of benefits, assuming every worker qualifies immediately","The start date, assuming coverage is identical across employers","A coworker’s coverage, assuming it defines the applicant’s entitlement"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Check which benefits apply to you, their coverage and their starting date. Part-time eligibility and waiting periods can differ, so do not assume benefits are automatic.","source":"Chapter 13 — Benefits; Day 4 presentation, slide 29","difficulty":1,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Benefits","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_17"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 29","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_17"}],"originalId":"c13_17","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_19","chapter":13,"mode":"level1","topic":"Career knowledge","prompt":"A student plans to wait for an appealing vacancy before preparing a résumé. Which approach better supports a prompt, tailored application?","choices":["Prepare a master résumé now, then adapt relevant information to the posting.","Write a different employment history for each opening once it appears.","Wait for an interview invitation so the employer can specify the résumé content.","Prepare one fixed version and send it unchanged to every employer."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Preparing a résumé before beginning the search makes it easier to respond promptly while tailoring relevant, truthful qualifications.","source":"Chapter 13 — Resumé; Day 4 presentation, slide 5","difficulty":1,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Resumé","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_19"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 5","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_19"}],"originalId":"c13_19","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_31","chapter":13,"mode":"level1","topic":"Career knowledge","prompt":"An applicant wants a friend to accompany them to an interview for reassurance. What is the most professional default plan, apart from an agreed accommodation?","choices":["Arrange support outside the interview venue and attend the interview independently.","Bring the friend into reception and let them answer questions about the applicant.","Bring the friend into the interview if they promise to remain silent.","Ask the friend to explain the applicant’s nervousness before the interview starts."],"answer":[0],"explain":"The course advises attending independently rather than bringing a friend to the interview venue. An agreed accommodation is a separate arrangement and should not be confused with a casual companion.","source":"Chapter 13 — Making a Good Impression — Conduct; Day 4 presentation, slide 23","difficulty":1,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Making a Good Impression — Conduct","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_31"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 23","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_31"}],"originalId":"c13_31","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_35","chapter":13,"mode":"level1","topic":"Career knowledge","prompt":"An interviewer introduces themselves using a formal title and surname. How should the applicant address them initially?","choices":["Use the introduced form unless invited to use another.","Use their first name immediately to establish confidence.","Avoid using any name even when a greeting calls for one.","Use the form preferred by a friend who works at the agency."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Do not assume first-name familiarity. Follow the interviewer’s introduction and any invitation to use another form of address.","source":"Chapter 13 — Interview Tips; Day 4 presentation, slide 25","difficulty":1,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Interview Tips","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_35"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 25","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_35"}],"originalId":"c13_35","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_39","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Résumés & portfolios","prompt":"A new graduate has strong placement skills but little paid care experience. Which format best matches this situation?","choices":["A functional résumé with skills emphasized and dated work history included","A chronological résumé with placements omitted","A functional résumé with employment dates removed","A CV that replaces all employment details"],"answer":[0],"explain":"A functional résumé emphasizes skills and is useful with little experience in the chosen field. Keep a brief, dated employment history; a skills focus is not a reason to hide dates.","source":"Chapter 13 — Functional Resumé; Day 4 presentation, slide 6","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Functional Resumé","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_39"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 6","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_39"}],"originalId":"c13_39","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_40","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Résumés & portfolios","prompt":"An applicant has six continuous years of care work and few employment gaps. Which format best showcases that record?","choices":["Chronological, with the latest employment first","Functional, with all dates removed","Chronological, beginning with the oldest position","Portfolio only, without a résumé"],"answer":[0],"explain":"A chronological résumé makes a steady employment record easy to follow, beginning with the latest position. It does not start with the oldest job or replace the résumé with a portfolio.","source":"Chapter 13 — Chronological Resumé; Day 4 presentation, slide 6","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Chronological Resumé","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_40"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 6","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_40"}],"originalId":"c13_40","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_42","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Résumés & portfolios","prompt":"A graduate says unpaid placement experience does not belong on a résumé. Which response is most accurate?","choices":["Relevant placements and volunteer work can demonstrate qualifications","Only paid employment demonstrates useful experience","Placements belong only in the cover letter","Volunteer work counts only after full-time employment"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Experience can come from placements, volunteering, projects and other relevant activities, not just paid work. Explain the skills and accomplishments that demonstrate suitability.","source":"Chapter 13 — Elements of a Resumé; Day 4 presentation, slide 4","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Elements of a Resumé","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_42"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 4","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_42"}],"originalId":"c13_42","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_43","chapter":13,"mode":"level1","topic":"Résumés & portfolios","prompt":"Which education details should a résumé include?","choices":["Exact certificate name, institution and graduation year","School name and a general statement about health care","Expected job title and a list of personal interests","Certificate name without the institution or year"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Identify the exact certificate, institution and graduation year. Relevant additional training and awards may be included; a broad statement about health care does not identify the qualification.","source":"Chapter 13 — Elements of a Resumé; Day 4 presentation, slide 4","difficulty":1,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Elements of a Resumé","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_43"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 4","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_43"}],"originalId":"c13_43","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_45","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Résumés & portfolios","prompt":"An applicant cannot identify any professional strengths. What should they do first?","choices":["Review experiences and identify what each taught them","Copy a list of another applicant’s strengths","Choose qualities that sound impressive without evidence","Leave all skills out until they have paid experience"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Start with real experiences and ask what you learned from each. This produces evidence of your own skills rather than copied strengths or unsupported claims.","source":"Chapter 13 — Getting Started on Your Resumé; Day 4 presentation, slide 5","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Getting Started on Your Resumé","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_45"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 5","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_45"}],"originalId":"c13_45","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_46","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Résumés & portfolios","prompt":"Two jobs require different skills. How should the applicant use a master résumé?","choices":["Adapt relevant qualifications for each posting","Change job titles to match each posting","Use one version because facts cannot be tailored","Remove education from whichever version is longer"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Use the same truthful history while changing which relevant qualifications you emphasize for each posting. Tailoring does not permit changing job titles or inventing credentials.","source":"Chapter 13 — Creating Your Resumé; Day 4 presentation, slide 7","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Creating Your Resumé","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_46"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 7","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_46"}],"originalId":"c13_46","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_47","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Résumés & portfolios","prompt":"An applicant exaggerates a placement role to appear experienced. What is the best correction?","choices":["Use the actual role and describe genuine accomplishments","Keep the title but reduce the number of duties listed","Keep the claim if a reference is unlikely to check it","Move the exaggerated statement to the cover letter"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Use the actual role and genuine accomplishments. Exaggeration remains misleading in a résumé or cover letter, even if a reference might not be asked to verify it.","source":"Chapter 13 — Creating Your Resumé; Day 4 presentation, slide 7","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Creating Your Resumé","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_47"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 7","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_47"}],"originalId":"c13_47","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_48","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Résumés & portfolios","prompt":"A résumé uses long paragraphs beginning with “I was responsible for.” Which revision makes the qualifications easier to scan?","choices":["Short, direct bullet points beginning with action words","Longer paragraphs with more technical vocabulary","Brief paragraphs that repeat the same responsibility","A separate page explaining every routine duty"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Short action-led bullet points make qualifications easier to scan. Long duty descriptions and unnecessary jargon can hide the relevant skills and results.","source":"Chapter 13 — Creating Your Resumé; Day 4 presentation, slide 7","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Creating Your Resumé","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_48"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 7","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_48"}],"originalId":"c13_48","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_49","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Résumés & portfolios","prompt":"Spell-check finds no errors, but the résumé uses “their” where “there” belongs. What does this illustrate?","choices":["Automated checking does not replace careful proofreading","Correct spelling guarantees correct meaning","Only another software program can check the résumé","Word choice matters only in the cover letter"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Spell-check can miss correctly spelled words used in the wrong context. Read for meaning and ask reliable people to review the document; software alone cannot establish accuracy.","source":"Chapter 13 — Creating Your Resumé; Day 4 presentation, slide 7","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Creating Your Resumé","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_49"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 7","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_49"}],"originalId":"c13_49","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_51","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Résumés & portfolios","prompt":"A résumé alternates fonts, bullet styles and verb forms. What needs attention?","choices":["Consistency of formatting and grammar","The absence of a separate interests page","The need to remove all headings","The need to add a second typeface"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Consistent fonts, headings, bullets, spelling conventions and verb forms make information easier to follow. Adding another typeface would increase the inconsistency.","source":"Chapter 13 — Creating Your Resumé; Day 4 presentation, slide 7","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Creating Your Resumé","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_51"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 7","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_51"}],"originalId":"c13_51","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_52","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Résumés & portfolios","prompt":"A two-page résumé feels crowded. Which revision best reflects the formatting advice?","choices":["Remove irrelevant details and retain a simple, readable format","Reduce the text to an unusually small font","Use decorative fonts to separate sections","Replace white space with additional graphics"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Remove irrelevant material and preserve readable spacing and type. Tiny text, decorative fonts and extra graphics may fit more content but reduce readability.","source":"Chapter 13 — Formatting and Printing Your Resumé; Day 4 presentation, slide 8","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Formatting and Printing Your Resumé","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_52"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 8","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_52"}],"originalId":"c13_52","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_53","chapter":13,"mode":"level1","topic":"Résumés & portfolios","prompt":"A candidate includes both an objective and a career profile. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["Both are optional, and most people choose one rather than both","Every résumé must include both","A profile is required only for unpaid experience","An objective replaces the education section"],"answer":[0],"explain":"An objective and a career profile are optional, and most applicants use one rather than both. Neither replaces the core information about experience and education.","source":"Chapter 13 — Elements of a Resumé; Day 4 presentation, slide 4","difficulty":1,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Elements of a Resumé","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_53"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 4","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_53"}],"originalId":"c13_53","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_54","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Résumés & portfolios","prompt":"When is an interest most useful in an application?","choices":["When it demonstrates a skill relevant to the job","When it fills otherwise empty space","When it is unrelated but unusual","When it replaces evidence from a placement"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Include an interest when it demonstrates a relevant skill, such as teamwork. An unrelated hobby should not occupy space needed for stronger evidence of qualifications.","source":"Chapter 13 — Elements of a Resumé; Day 4 presentation, slide 4","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Elements of a Resumé","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_54"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 4","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_54"}],"originalId":"c13_54","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_55","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Résumés & portfolios","prompt":"An interviewer wants to locate the applicant’s newest certificate quickly. How should the portfolio be arranged?","choices":["Organized categories with recent items first and labelled dividers","Oldest documents first with no categories","Only original documents in the order they were found","A single unlabelled collection sorted by page size"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Use clearly labelled categories and place recent evidence first. Organization helps an interviewer locate a current certificate without searching an unlabelled collection.","source":"Chapter 13 — The Professional Portfolio; Hardcopy Portfolio; Day 4 presentation, slide 9","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · The Professional Portfolio; Hardcopy Portfolio","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_55"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 9","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_55"}],"originalId":"c13_55","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_56","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Résumés & portfolios","prompt":"An employer asks for the portfolio before the interview. What is the best preparation?","choices":["An ordered electronic file or link containing carefully scanned items","Photographs of random binder pages sent separately","A résumé alone because portfolios must be physical","A list of certificates without supporting documents"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Scan relevant documents clearly and organize the electronic portfolio in an accessible file or link. A résumé alone does not provide the supporting evidence requested.","source":"Chapter 13 — Electronic Portfolio; Day 4 presentation, slide 10","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Electronic Portfolio","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_56"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 10","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_56"}],"originalId":"c13_56","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_57","chapter":13,"mode":"level1","topic":"Résumés & portfolios","prompt":"Why bring photocopies as well as portfolio originals?","choices":["The interviewer may wish to retain selected copies","The interviewer must keep every original certificate","Copies replace the need to organize the binder","Original documents should never be shown"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Bring copies that the interviewer may retain while you keep your original documents. Copies supplement an organized portfolio rather than replace it.","source":"Chapter 13 — Hardcopy Portfolio; Day 4 presentation, slide 10","difficulty":1,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Hardcopy Portfolio","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_57"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 10","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_57"}],"originalId":"c13_57","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_58","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Résumés & portfolios","prompt":"A résumé was updated after a new course, but the backup is old. What should the applicant do?","choices":["Update accessible electronic copies and backups","Keep only the printed version","Wait until an interview is offered","Delete the backup to avoid version differences"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Update the working résumé and its accessible backups after new training. An old backup can lead to submitting outdated information when away from your usual computer.","source":"Chapter 13 — Resumé; Day 4 presentation, slide 5","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Resumé","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_58"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 5","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_58"}],"originalId":"c13_58","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_59","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Job searches & applications","prompt":"A student is unsure whether to seek facility or community work. What should guide the search first?","choices":["Clarify preferred setting, work type and employment goals","Apply only to the first opening seen","Choose a résumé font before considering any goals","Assume self-employment is the only flexible choice"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Clarify the type of work, preferred setting and full-time, part-time or self-employment goals. These priorities help focus the search before choosing individual vacancies.","source":"Chapter 13 — Setting Priorities and Goals; Day 4 presentation, slide 3","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Setting Priorities and Goals","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_59"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 3","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_59"}],"originalId":"c13_59","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_60","chapter":13,"mode":"level1","topic":"Job searches & applications","prompt":"A candidate cannot afford a home computer. Which option is available during a job search?","choices":["Use access available through a public library","Limit the search to employers accepting verbal applications","Delay the search until a computer is purchased","Ask references to submit every application"],"answer":[0],"explain":"A public library may provide computer and printer access. Not owning a computer does not mean a job seeker must delay the search or rely only on verbal applications.","source":"Chapter 13 — Getting Organized; Day 4 presentation, slide 2","difficulty":1,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Getting Organized","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_60"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 2","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_60"}],"originalId":"c13_60","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_61","chapter":13,"mode":"level1","topic":"Job searches & applications","prompt":"An employer’s callback reaches a playful voicemail greeting. What should the applicant improve?","choices":["Set up a professional greeting and a reliable contact method","Keep the greeting and depend on the résumé attachment to establish professionalism","Disable voicemail so a callback must reach the applicant directly","List a reference’s number as the main contact without arranging this beforehand"],"answer":[0],"explain":"A clear, professional voicemail greeting and working contact method help an employer reach you. Removing voicemail can make missed calls harder to return.","source":"Chapter 13 — Getting Organized; Day 4 presentation, slide 2","difficulty":1,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Getting Organized","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_61"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 2","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_61"}],"originalId":"c13_61","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_62","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Job searches & applications","prompt":"A search returns jobs across the country rather than nearby support work. What is the best adjustment?","choices":["Use support-work keywords and the desired city or area","Search only the word “jobs”","Remove all location information","Use only general employer slogans"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Combine relevant role keywords with the desired city or area. General searches or removing location information will usually make results less focused.","source":"Chapter 13 — Finding and Following Leads; Day 4 presentation, slide 11","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Finding and Following Leads","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_62"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 11","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_62"}],"originalId":"c13_62","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_63","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Job searches & applications","prompt":"No suitable vacancies are advertised. Which action best addresses the hidden job market?","choices":["Ask instructors and placement contacts about possible openings","Resubmit the same application to an unrelated vacancy","Wait until a newspaper advertises the exact role","Remove the location limits without exploring contacts"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Instructors, placement employers and other professional contacts may know about internal or upcoming vacancies. Waiting only for advertisements overlooks these leads.","source":"Chapter 13 — Finding and Following Leads; Day 4 presentation, slide 12","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Finding and Following Leads","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_63"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 12","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_63"}],"originalId":"c13_63","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_64","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Job searches & applications","prompt":"A job seeker wants to identify local home-care agencies before approaching them. Which source fits?","choices":["The district home-care program’s agency information","A former client’s confidential care record","Only national advertisements for hospital physicians","A generic résumé template service"],"answer":[0],"explain":"A district home-care program can help identify agencies providing local services. Confidential client information is not a job-search resource.","source":"Chapter 13 — Finding and Following Leads; Day 4 presentation, slide 12","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Finding and Following Leads","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_64"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 12","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_64"}],"originalId":"c13_64","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_65","chapter":13,"mode":"level3","topic":"Job searches & applications","prompt":"A reference agreed to one application last month. A new employer now requests references. What should happen next?","choices":["Confirm permission for the new position and update the reference","Assume the earlier agreement covers every future application","List the reference but change the phone number to your own","Wait until after the employer has called"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Confirm that the person will provide a reference for this application and keep them informed. Permission for an earlier application should not be assumed to cover every future position.","source":"Chapter 13 — Selecting References; Day 4 presentation, slide 14","difficulty":3,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Selecting References","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_65"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 14","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_65"}],"originalId":"c13_65","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_66","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Job searches & applications","prompt":"A respected former supervisor agrees to be a reference. What else should the applicant clarify?","choices":["What the supervisor would say about strengths and weaknesses","Whether the supervisor will omit any weakness even when directly asked","Whether the supervisor will describe duties beyond the actual placement role","Whether the supervisor will give the same answer for every position without an update"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Ask what the person would say about your strengths and weaknesses so you understand the reference they can honestly provide. A reference cannot guarantee a job or replace the selection process.","source":"Chapter 13 — Selecting References; Day 4 presentation, slide 14","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Selecting References","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_66"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 14","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_66"}],"originalId":"c13_66","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_67","chapter":13,"mode":"level1","topic":"Job searches & applications","prompt":"Which reference-list entry is most useful to an interviewer?","choices":["Name, accurate contact details, title and relationship to the applicant","Name and personal hobbies","Name and a copied quotation without contact details","First name and social media nickname"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Include accurate contact details, the person’s professional title and how they know your work. A name or social nickname alone does not let an employer obtain a useful reference.","source":"Chapter 13 — The Professional Portfolio; Selecting References; Day 4 presentation, slide 9","difficulty":1,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · The Professional Portfolio; Selecting References","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_67"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 9","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_67"}],"originalId":"c13_67","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_68","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Job searches & applications","prompt":"A candidate did not receive the job. How should they handle their references afterward?","choices":["Update them on the outcome and maintain contact","Contact them only if they caused a problem","Remove them without explanation","Assume they do not want any further updates"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Keep references informed about the outcome and thank them for their help. Follow-up matters even when you were not offered the job.","source":"Chapter 13 — Selecting References; Day 4 presentation, slide 14","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Selecting References","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_68"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 14","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_68"}],"originalId":"c13_68","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_69","chapter":13,"mode":"level3","topic":"Job searches & applications","prompt":"A cover letter repeats every résumé duty in paragraph form. What is the strongest revision?","choices":["Explain how selected skills and experience meet this employer’s needs","Add more duties so the résumé becomes unnecessary","Remove all examples to make it sound confident","Replace the letter with a list of references"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Connect selected skills and accomplishments to this employer’s needs. The letter should persuade the reader of your fit rather than repeat every résumé duty.","source":"Chapter 13 — Preparing a Letter of Application; Day 4 presentation, slide 15","difficulty":3,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Preparing a Letter of Application","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_69"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 15","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_69"}],"originalId":"c13_69","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_70","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Job searches & applications","prompt":"Before writing an unsolicited application, what should the applicant research?","choices":["The employer’s services, goals and practices","Only the salary offered by a different agency","Only the design of its logo","Which résumé font other applicants use"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Research the employer’s services, values and practices before approaching them. This lets you explain relevant fit even when no vacancy has been advertised.","source":"Chapter 13 — Determining the Employer’s Needs; Day 4 presentation, slide 15","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Determining the Employer’s Needs","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_70"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 15","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_70"}],"originalId":"c13_70","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_71","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Job searches & applications","prompt":"A posting names a recruitment contact. To whom should the letter be directed?","choices":["The designated hiring contact","The highest-ranking administrator regardless of the posting","A current client at the facility","Every manager in separate identical messages"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Address the person or department designated for recruitment. Sending the same application to unrelated administrators does not make it more personalized.","source":"Chapter 13 — Preparing a Letter of Application; Day 4 presentation, slide 15","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Preparing a Letter of Application","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_71"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 15","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_71"}],"originalId":"c13_71","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_72","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Job searches & applications","prompt":"What belongs in the opening paragraph of a solicited cover letter?","choices":["The job sought, how it was found and the purpose of writing","A detailed list of all previous duties","A request for benefits information","A full explanation of every employment gap"],"answer":[0],"explain":"The opening identifies the position, how you learned of it and why you are writing. Details establishing your qualifications belong in the following paragraphs.","source":"Chapter 13 — Organizing a Letter of Application — Body; Day 4 presentation, slide 15","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Organizing a Letter of Application — Body","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_72"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 15","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_72"}],"originalId":"c13_72","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_73","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Job searches & applications","prompt":"Which closing gives a cover letter a clear next step?","choices":["Request an interview and refer to the enclosed résumé","Repeat the full employment history","Ask the reader to supply three references","End without identifying any next step"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Close by requesting an interview and referring to the résumé. An unsolicited letter may also state when you intend to follow up.","source":"Chapter 13 — Organizing a Letter of Application — Closing; Day 4 presentation, slide 15","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Organizing a Letter of Application — Closing","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_73"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 15","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_73"}],"originalId":"c13_73","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_74","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Job searches & applications","prompt":"A new graduate writes, “Sorry that I have almost no experience.” Which revision is strongest?","choices":["Describe relevant placement learning in a respectful, capable tone","Claim to have extensive independent care experience","Replace the apology with criticism of other graduates","Emphasize needing the job more than other applicants"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Present genuine placement learning in a capable, respectful tone. Avoid apologizing for limited experience, inventing experience or criticizing other applicants.","source":"Chapter 13 — Writing Your Letter of Application; Day 4 presentation, slide 15","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Writing Your Letter of Application","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_74"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 15","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_74"}],"originalId":"c13_74","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_75","chapter":13,"mode":"level3","topic":"Job searches & applications","prompt":"An employer specifies a file format and says not to send a paper copy. What should the applicant do?","choices":["Use the specified format and follow the no-paper instruction","Send every available format plus a hard copy","Send a paper copy as well, despite the instruction","Use the applicant’s preferred format instead"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Follow the employer’s required file format and submission instructions. Sending additional formats or paper against an explicit request demonstrates poor attention to instructions.","source":"Chapter 13 — Delivering a Hard Copy of Your Letter of Application and Resumé; Day 4 presentation, slide 16","difficulty":3,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Delivering a Hard Copy of Your Letter of Application and Resumé","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_75"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 16","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_75"}],"originalId":"c13_75","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_76","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Job searches & applications","prompt":"A résumé’s layout changes when opened on another computer. If no format is specified, which option addresses this problem?","choices":["A PDF version that preserves formatting","A screenshot pasted into a social media message","A decorative email background","An additional reference list without the résumé"],"answer":[0],"explain":"PDF can preserve the layout across computers. Follow any format the employer specifies; a screenshot or unrelated attachment does not solve the résumé-formatting problem.","source":"Chapter 13 — Delivering a Hard Copy of Your Letter of Application and Resumé; Day 4 presentation, slide 16","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Delivering a Hard Copy of Your Letter of Application and Resumé","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_76"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 16","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_76"}],"originalId":"c13_76","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_77","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Job searches & applications","prompt":"An application email has a professional attachment but uses “CU later” in the message. What needs revision?","choices":["Use full sentences, correct grammar and professional language","Only rename the attachment","Only change the email background","Add more abbreviations to keep a consistent style"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Use complete sentences, appropriate spelling and professional language in the email as well as the attachments. Fixing only the file name or background leaves the message unprofessional.","source":"Chapter 13 — Tips for Conveying Professionalism Electronically; Day 4 presentation, slide 16","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Tips for Conveying Professionalism Electronically","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_77"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 16","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_77"}],"originalId":"c13_77","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_78","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Job searches & applications","prompt":"A job form repeats information already in the résumé. What is the best approach?","choices":["Complete requested fields carefully and attach the résumé as appropriate","Leave repeated sections blank","Write only “see résumé” in every field","Complete only contact details and sign"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Complete requested fields accurately even when the résumé contains the same information. Application forms collect specific details and can demonstrate reading, writing and attention to instructions.","source":"Chapter 13 — Completing a Job Application Form; Day 4 presentation, slide 17","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Completing a Job Application Form","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_78"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 17","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_78"}],"originalId":"c13_78","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_79","chapter":13,"mode":"level3","topic":"Job searches & applications","prompt":"An application asks why the applicant left a job from which they were dismissed. What should they do?","choices":["Answer truthfully and add an explanation if needed","Describe the dismissal as voluntary resignation","Omit the job while leaving misleading dates","Ask a reference to provide a different account"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Give a truthful reason for leaving and explain the circumstances if appropriate. Calling a dismissal a resignation or arranging a false reference misrepresents the history.","source":"Chapter 13 — Completing a Job Application Form; Day 4 presentation, slide 17","difficulty":3,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Completing a Job Application Form","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_79"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 17","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_79"}],"originalId":"c13_79","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_80","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Job searches & applications","prompt":"A candidate posts work availability on social media. How can they remain reachable while protecting their privacy?","choices":["Provide appropriate contact access while managing privacy and professionalism","Publish all identifying information to appear open","Assume privacy settings make all content irrelevant to employers","Focus on appearance while ignoring spelling"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Make appropriate contact possible while managing privacy and professional presentation. Privacy settings do not justify careless public content or unnecessary disclosure of identifying details.","source":"Chapter 13 — Using Social Networking Sites; Day 4 presentation, slide 18","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Using Social Networking Sites","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_80"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 18","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_80"}],"originalId":"c13_80","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_82","chapter":13,"mode":"level1","topic":"Interviews & employment offers","prompt":"An interviewer asks what the candidate would do in an imaginary situation. What is being assessed?","choices":["Application of learning and problem-solving ability","Recall of the employer’s address only","Whether the candidate memorized a résumé template","Ability to avoid explaining a decision"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Situational questions test how you apply learning and solve problems, not just recall facts. Explain a sensible response to the situation rather than avoiding the decision.","source":"Chapter 13 — The Interview; Day 4 presentation, slide 20","difficulty":1,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · The Interview","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_82"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 20","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_82"}],"originalId":"c13_82","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_83","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Interviews & employment offers","prompt":"A candidate prepares facts about the agency but no examples about themselves. What preparation is missing?","choices":["Specific experiences illustrating skills, strengths and weaknesses","A second printed copy of the agency website","A list of unrelated hobbies for small talk","A statement that they have no weaknesses"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Prepare specific experiences illustrating your skills, strengths and weaknesses as well as researching the agency. Employer facts alone do not demonstrate your own suitability.","source":"Chapter 13 — The Interview — Preparation; Day 4 presentation, slide 19","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · The Interview — Preparation","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_83"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 19","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_83"}],"originalId":"c13_83","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_84","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Interviews & employment offers","prompt":"Several applicants are answering questions in the same room. What most clearly demonstrates teamwork?","choices":["Listen attentively, respond politely and avoid interrupting","Answer over others to show confidence","Look away whenever another applicant speaks","Critique each answer before giving your own"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Listen to other applicants, respond politely and avoid interrupting. The interviewer can observe teamwork and communication while comparing answers.","source":"Chapter 13 — The Group Interview — More Than One Applicant; Day 4 presentation, slide 20","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · The Group Interview — More Than One Applicant","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_84"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 20","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_84"}],"originalId":"c13_84","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_85","chapter":13,"mode":"level3","topic":"Interviews & employment offers","prompt":"Two interviewers take turns asking prepared and follow-up questions. How should the candidate respond?","choices":["Attend to the speaker and answer honestly with appropriate body language","Address only the person who appears most senior","Repeat one memorized answer regardless of the question","Treat follow-up questions as evidence of rejection"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Attend to the interviewer asking the question and answer sincerely. Follow-up questions seek clarification and do not by themselves signal rejection.","source":"Chapter 13 — The Group Interview — More Than One Interviewer; Day 4 presentation, slide 20","difficulty":3,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · The Group Interview — More Than One Interviewer","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_85"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 20","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_85"}],"originalId":"c13_85","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_86","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Interviews & employment offers","prompt":"A question is unclear, but the candidate worries that asking will look weak. What should they do?","choices":["Ask for clarification before giving a focused response","Answer a similar question they had rehearsed","Agree with the interviewer without answering","Give a very long response covering many topics"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Ask for clarification when a question is unclear. Answering a different rehearsed question or covering many unrelated topics does not demonstrate accurate listening.","source":"Chapter 13 — Developing Listening Skills; Day 4 presentation, slide 25","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Developing Listening Skills","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_86"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 25","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_86"}],"originalId":"c13_86","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_87","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Interviews & employment offers","prompt":"A nervous candidate answers before the interviewer finishes. What adjustment would help most?","choices":["Listen to the full question, pause and think before responding","Speak faster to finish before becoming anxious","Fill every pause with more information","Avoid all eye contact to concentrate"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Listen to the whole question, pause and think before answering. Rushing or filling every silence can reduce the relevance and clarity of your response.","source":"Chapter 13 — Developing Listening Skills; Interview Tips; Day 4 presentation, slide 25","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Developing Listening Skills; Interview Tips","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_87"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 25","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_87"}],"originalId":"c13_87","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_88","chapter":13,"mode":"level3","topic":"Interviews & employment offers","prompt":"A candidate has held several jobs for less than a year. What concern should they prepare to address?","choices":["Commitment and the reasons for the employment pattern","Whether to remove all short jobs even when a complete history is requested","Whether the most recent employer can replace every earlier reference","Whether changing the date format will make the employment pattern irrelevant"],"answer":[0],"explain":"A series of short jobs may raise questions about commitment. Prepare an honest explanation instead of hiding employment or assuming the interviewer will not notice the pattern.","source":"Chapter 13 — The Interview — Preparation; Day 4 presentation, slide 19","difficulty":3,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · The Interview — Preparation","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_88"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 19","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_88"}],"originalId":"c13_88","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_89","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Interviews & employment offers","prompt":"The interview venue is unfamiliar and traffic is unpredictable. What is the best preparation?","choices":["Practise the route and allow extra travel time","Arrive in the building exactly at the scheduled minute","Leave earlier only if traffic is already visibly delayed","Ask the interviewer to call when the room is ready"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Check the route in advance and allow extra time for delays. Aim to reach reception 5–10 minutes early rather than arriving at the scheduled minute.","source":"Chapter 13 — Planning; Making a Good Impression — Conduct; Day 4 presentation, slide 23","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Planning; Making a Good Impression — Conduct","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_89"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 23","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_89"}],"originalId":"c13_89","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_90","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Interviews & employment offers","prompt":"A virtual interview starts in ten minutes. Which preparation is most appropriate?","choices":["Be ready early in a setting where interruptions are controlled","Join at the exact start time from a busy shared room","Keep messaging friends until called on","Treat grooming and conduct as less relevant online"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Be ready early in a quiet setting where interruptions are controlled. Professional grooming, preparation and conduct apply to virtual interviews too.","source":"Chapter 13 — Making a Good Impression — Conduct; Day 4 presentation, slide 23","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Making a Good Impression — Conduct","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_90"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 23","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_90"}],"originalId":"c13_90","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_91","chapter":13,"mode":"level3","topic":"Interviews & employment offers","prompt":"Asked about a former employer, the candidate feels tempted to complain. What is the strongest response?","choices":["Discuss the experience honestly and professionally without ridicule","Conceal the employment entirely","Use the opportunity to list every disliked coworker","Give praise that contradicts the application history"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Discuss the previous job honestly and constructively without ridicule. Being positive does not require inventing praise or concealing relevant history.","source":"Chapter 13 — Interview Tips; Day 4 presentation, slide 25","difficulty":3,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Interview Tips","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_91"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 25","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_91"}],"originalId":"c13_91","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_93","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Interviews & employment offers","prompt":"The interviewer has already answered every prepared question. What is an appropriate closing response?","choices":["Say the discussion has addressed the questions you prepared","Ask the same questions again to prove preparation","Invent a question unrelated to the role","Leave without thanking the interviewer"],"answer":[0],"explain":"It is acceptable to say that your prepared questions have already been answered. Do not repeat them unnecessarily or invent an irrelevant question just to speak.","source":"Chapter 13 — Interview Tips — Ask the Right Questions; Day 4 presentation, slide 26","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Interview Tips — Ask the Right Questions","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_93"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 26","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_93"}],"originalId":"c13_93","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_95","chapter":13,"mode":"level1","topic":"Interviews & employment offers","prompt":"When should you send a thank-you message after an interview?","choices":["As soon as possible, no later than the following day","Only after receiving a rejection","At the end of the first month of employment","After the employer contacts every reference"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Send thanks promptly, ideally no later than the next day. Reinforce your interest; waiting for a decision loses the chance to provide timely follow-up.","source":"Chapter 13 — Follow-up; Day 4 presentation, slide 27","difficulty":1,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Follow-up","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_95"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 27","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_95"}],"originalId":"c13_95","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_96","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Interviews & employment offers","prompt":"A candidate has heard nothing after an interview. What should they do while waiting?","choices":["Continue exploring other opportunities","Stop applying until a rejection arrives","Assume silence confirms an offer","Tell references the job has been secured"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Continue exploring opportunities while awaiting a decision. Silence confirms neither an offer nor a rejection, and references should not be told the job is secured.","source":"Chapter 13 — The Employment Offer; Day 4 presentation, slide 28","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · The Employment Offer","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_96"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 28","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_96"}],"originalId":"c13_96","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_97","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Interviews & employment offers","prompt":"An unsuccessful applicant wants feedback. Which approach best supports learning?","choices":["Ask courteously for feedback without sounding accusatory","Demand a comparison with the hired candidate","Ask the receptionist to overturn the decision","Send repeated complaints about the outcome"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Ask for feedback courteously as a learning opportunity. Complaints or demands to compare yourself with the successful applicant undermine that purpose.","source":"Chapter 13 — The Employment Offer; Day 4 presentation, slide 28","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · The Employment Offer","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_97"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 28","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_97"}],"originalId":"c13_97","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_98","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Interviews & employment offers","prompt":"An offer gives a job title but leaves reporting arrangements unclear. What should be clarified before starting?","choices":["Start date, pay, hours, reporting location and written expectations","Pay and start date, assuming the advertised duties settle every other term","Reporting location and uniform, leaving pay for the first shift","Job title and benefits, assuming the supervisor and hours will be unchanged"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Clarify start date, pay, hours, reporting arrangements and job expectations, and obtain the terms in writing. A title alone does not establish how employment will work.","source":"Chapter 13 — Accepting an Offer; Day 4 presentation, slide 28","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Accepting an Offer","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_98"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 28","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_98"}],"originalId":"c13_98","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_99","chapter":13,"mode":"level1","topic":"Interviews & employment offers","prompt":"What is the main purpose of a probationary period?","choices":["A trial period for both worker and employer to assess fit","A period that automatically satisfies outstanding pre-employment conditions","A period in which job duties remain undefined until assessment ends","A guarantee that all benefits begin before eligibility is checked"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Probation is a trial period in which both worker and employer assess suitability. It does not replace conditional requirements or guarantee immediate benefit eligibility.","source":"Chapter 13 — Probation; Day 4 presentation, slide 29","difficulty":1,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Probation","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_99"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 29","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_99"}],"originalId":"c13_99","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:c13_100","chapter":13,"mode":"level3","topic":"Interviews & employment offers","prompt":"A worker is leaving because of workplace concerns. Which explanation is most constructive?","choices":["A tactful, specific account that also acknowledges positive aspects","A general statement that all staff are terrible","An exaggerated story intended to gain sympathy","An ultimatum delivered without arranging a discussion"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Discuss specific concerns honestly and tactfully, acknowledge positive aspects and maintain professionalism. General insults or exaggerated complaints provide less useful information.","source":"Chapter 13 — How to Refuse or Leave a Job Without Leaving a Negative Impression; Day 4 presentation, slide 31","difficulty":3,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · How to Refuse or Leave a Job Without Leaving a Negative Impression","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_100"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 31","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#c13_100"}],"originalId":"c13_100","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:wb13_02","chapter":13,"mode":"level1","topic":"Career decisions","prompt":"An employer asks for a “letter of application” alongside the résumé. Which document should the applicant prepare?","choices":["A cover letter linking relevant qualifications to the position","A reference list describing people who can confirm work performance","A CV containing every academic achievement","An acceptance letter confirming the employment terms"],"answer":[0],"explain":"A letter of application is a cover letter. It introduces the application and explains the fit rather than replacing the résumé, references or acceptance.","source":"Chapter 13 — Preparing a Letter of Application; Day 4 presentation, slide 15","difficulty":1,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Preparing a Letter of Application","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#wb13_02"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 15","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#wb13_02"}],"originalId":"wb13_02","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:wb13_03","chapter":13,"mode":"level1","topic":"Career decisions","prompt":"A cover letter runs to more than two pages because it repeats each résumé duty. Which revision best follows the course’s advice?","choices":["Keep a concise letter focused on selected evidence of fit with the employer’s needs.","Keep the length and add the complete reference list to make the letter comprehensive.","Remove the résumé attachment so the long letter can serve both purposes.","Reduce the font substantially while retaining every repeated detail."],"answer":[0],"explain":"The letter should be concise and purposeful. Repeating the full résumé or shrinking text does not improve relevance or readability.","source":"Chapter 13 — Writing Your Letter of Application; Day 4 presentation, slide 15","difficulty":1,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Writing Your Letter of Application","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#wb13_03"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 15","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#wb13_03"}],"originalId":"wb13_03","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:wb13_12","chapter":13,"mode":"level3","topic":"Career decisions","prompt":"Which checks directly assess the quality and integrity of a résumé?","choices":["Relevance of the information","Honesty of the information","Clarity of expression","Consistency of presentation","Whether it guarantees a job offer"],"answer":[0,1,2,3],"explain":"Check relevance, honesty, clarity and consistency. Even an excellent résumé cannot guarantee an offer.","source":"Chapter 13 — Creating Your Resumé; Day 4 presentation, slide 7","difficulty":3,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Creating Your Resumé","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#wb13_12"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 7","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#wb13_12"}],"originalId":"wb13_12","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:wb13_13","chapter":13,"mode":"level3","topic":"Career decisions","prompt":"Which are established ways to find job leads? Select all that apply.","choices":["Employment advertisements on social media sites","Flyers found on windshields","The Internet","College career services","Personal contacts"],"answer":[0,2,3,4],"explain":"Use employment advertisements, online searches, college career services and personal contacts. The supplied job-search guidance does not identify anonymous windshield flyers as a recommended lead. Verify any employer before sharing personal information.","source":"Chapter 13 — Finding and Following Leads; Day 4 presentation, slide 11","difficulty":3,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Finding and Following Leads","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#wb13_13"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 11","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#wb13_13"}],"originalId":"wb13_13","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:wb13_14","chapter":13,"mode":"level3","topic":"Career decisions","prompt":"Which elements support a professional cover letter?","choices":["Relevant skills and qualities","Relevant accomplishments","Unrelated contest results","A respectful, capable tone","Accurate contact information"],"answer":[0,1,3,4],"explain":"Use relevant skills and accomplishments, a professional tone and accurate contact information. Unrelated contest results do not help demonstrate fit.","source":"Chapter 13 — Writing Your Letter of Application; Day 4 presentation, slide 15","difficulty":3,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Writing Your Letter of Application","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#wb13_14"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 15","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#wb13_14"}],"originalId":"wb13_14","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:wb13_15","chapter":13,"mode":"level3","topic":"Career decisions","prompt":"In an interview, what is the employer looking for?","choices":["Someone with the educational qualifications for the job","Someone who has the skills necessary to do the job or who is able to learn these skills","Someone to whom clients and co-workers will respond well","Someone who is reliable, responsible, and motivated","Someone selected primarily for physical attractiveness"],"answer":[0,1,2,3],"explain":"Interviewers assess education, skills or capacity to learn, working relationships, reliability and motivation. Physical attractiveness is not a job qualification.","source":"Chapter 13 — The Interview; Day 4 presentation, slide 19","difficulty":3,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · The Interview","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#wb13_15"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 19","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#wb13_15"}],"originalId":"wb13_15","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:wb13_16","chapter":13,"mode":"level3","topic":"Career decisions","prompt":"Which actions belong in a thorough interview preparation plan? Select all that apply.","choices":["Practise possible answers using notes or cue cards","Choose clean, professional clothing","Practise listening and relaxation techniques","Plan travel and prepare your résumé and portfolio","Memorize answers so you can ignore changes in the questions"],"answer":[0,1,2,3],"explain":"Prepare examples and possible answers, practise listening and relaxation, choose suitable clothing and plan travel and documents. Cue cards can help during practice; reading a script throughout the interview is different.","source":"Chapter 13 — Practising for Your Job Interview; Planning; Day 4 presentation, slide 19","difficulty":3,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Practising for Your Job Interview; Planning","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#wb13_16"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 19","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#wb13_16"}],"originalId":"wb13_16","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:wb13_17","chapter":13,"mode":"level3","topic":"Career decisions","prompt":"Which behaviours support clear, professional communication during an interview? Select all that apply.","choices":["Listen to the complete question","Answer before the interviewer finishes","Ask for clarification when needed","Use relevant experiences to support your answers","Describe previous employers with insults","Answer honestly and take time to think"],"answer":[0,2,3,5],"explain":"Listen, pause, answer honestly, support claims with examples and show respectful confidence. Rushing answers undermines clarity; familiarity does not justify assuming first-name use.","source":"Chapter 13 — Interview Tips; Day 4 presentation, slide 25","difficulty":3,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Interview Tips","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#wb13_17"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 25","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#wb13_17"}],"originalId":"wb13_17","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:wb13_18","chapter":13,"mode":"level3","topic":"Career decisions","prompt":"Which employment terms should you clarify before accepting an offer?","choices":["Conditions that must be met before employment","Whether a trial period applies","The pay offered and whether it can be negotiated","Whether you can postpone learning the job expectations until after accepting"],"answer":[0,1,2],"explain":"Clarify conditions, probation and compensation before accepting. You may ask whether pay is negotiable, but an employer is not obliged to negotiate.","source":"Chapter 13 — Accepting an Offer; Day 4 presentation, slide 29","difficulty":3,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Accepting an Offer","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#wb13_18"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 29","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#wb13_18"}],"originalId":"wb13_18","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:wb13_19","chapter":13,"mode":"level3","topic":"Career decisions","prompt":"You have had a job interview and are now planning to write a thank-you note or email to your interviewer. What points should your note include?","choices":["You should express your thanks for the interview.","You should show your interest in the position.","You should sign your name.","You should state your impressions about the interviewer."],"answer":[0,1,2],"explain":"Thank the interviewer, reaffirm interest and sign your name. Personal judgments about the interviewer are not the purpose of the message.","source":"Chapter 13 — Follow-up; Day 4 presentation, slide 27","difficulty":3,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Follow-up","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#wb13_19"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 27","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#wb13_19"}],"originalId":"wb13_19","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:wb13_20","chapter":13,"mode":"level1","topic":"Career decisions","prompt":"An applicant is preparing for an in-person interview in a care setting. Which choice best reflects professional preparation?","choices":["Avoid heavy perfume or aftershave and arrive with time to check in calmly.","Use a familiar strong scent to feel confident and arrive at the exact interview minute.","Choose clothing for a workout because the job includes physical tasks.","Plan to finish grooming in reception while waiting for the interviewer."],"answer":[0],"explain":"The course recommends avoiding heavy fragrances and preparing to arrive early and professionally.","source":"Chapter 13 — Making a Good Impression — Grooming, Clothing and Conduct; Day 4 presentation, slide 21","difficulty":1,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Making a Good Impression — Grooming, Clothing and Conduct","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#wb13_20"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 21","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#wb13_20"}],"originalId":"wb13_20","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:wb13_21","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Career decisions","prompt":"An application requests complete employment history. Mary deliberately leaves out two of her four jobs from the past year. What is this?","choices":["Unnecessary information","Invasion of privacy","Deception by omission","Ethically correct"],"answer":[2],"explain":"If the form requests complete employment history, deliberately leaving out jobs is deception by omission. A résumé may select relevant detail, but a requested complete history must be accurate.","source":"Chapter 13 — Completing a Job Application Form; Day 4 presentation, slide 17","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Completing a Job Application Form","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#wb13_21"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 17","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#wb13_21"}],"originalId":"wb13_21","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:wb13_22","chapter":13,"mode":"level1","topic":"Career decisions","prompt":"An application does not specify a reference count. According to the course preparation advice, how many references should the applicant be ready to provide?","choices":["Three, while following any different instructions from the employer","One, regardless of the employer’s instructions","Two, because a third reference is never appropriate","Four, because every application requires the same count"],"answer":[0],"explain":"The course recommends being prepared with three references. The employer’s actual application instructions take precedence over a general preparation figure.","source":"Chapter 13 — The Interview — Preparation; Day 4 presentation, slide 19","difficulty":1,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · The Interview — Preparation","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#wb13_22"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 19","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#wb13_22"}],"originalId":"wb13_22","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"chapter-13/data.js:wb13_23","chapter":13,"mode":"level2","topic":"Career decisions","prompt":"An applicant arrives early while reception staff are busy. Which behaviour best respects the workplace and keeps the applicant ready?","choices":["Check in appropriately and wait quietly without distracting the staff.","Begin a social conversation with the receptionist to demonstrate friendliness.","Make a phone call about the application where waiting visitors can hear it.","Move into the interview area without checking in so staff are not interrupted."],"answer":[0],"explain":"After appropriate check-in, wait calmly and respectfully. Professionalism includes how the applicant treats reception staff and shared spaces.","source":"Chapter 13 — Making a Good Impression — Conduct; Day 4 presentation, slide 23","difficulty":2,"references":[{"title":"Chapter 13 · Making a Good Impression — Conduct","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#wb13_23"},{"title":"Starting Your Career presentation · slide 23","href":"../chapter-13/sources.html#wb13_23"}],"originalId":"wb13_23","group":"Chapter 13","bank":"Chapter 13","sourceFiles":["chapter-13/data.js"]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_001","chapter":23,"mode":"level1","difficulty":1,"topic":"Electronic communication","prompt":"Which set consists entirely of electronic communication forms?","choices":["Webinars, patient portals, blogs","Case conferences in one room, blogs, hand gestures","Printed newsletters, podcasts, patient portals","Face-to-face interviews, email, webinars"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Electronic forms include webinars, portals and blogs. Printed material and in-person exchanges do not become electronic simply because they share information.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 3–4","originalId":"c23_001","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_002","chapter":23,"mode":"level1","difficulty":1,"topic":"Electronic communication","prompt":"What distinguishes a patient portal from a tablet?","choices":["The portal is a communication interface; the tablet is a device","The portal is a device; the tablet is a communication interface","Both are social networking accounts","Both are forms of public broadcasting"],"answer":[0],"explain":"A device provides access; an application or portal supplies the interface used to communicate.","source":"HCA200 Chapter 23 Quiz Preparation Notes · §2","originalId":"c23_002","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_003","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Electronic communication","prompt":"An educator sends one approved update to many authorized recipients. Which advantage is most directly demonstrated?","choices":["Cost-effective simultaneous distribution","Guaranteed individual understanding","Automatic confidentiality","Immediate personal feedback"],"answer":[0],"explain":"One message can reach multiple people efficiently. Delivery alone does not establish understanding, privacy or feedback.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 4","originalId":"c23_003","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_004","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Electronic communication","prompt":"A resident uses a tablet mainly to speak with distant family. What should guide staff support?","choices":["Recognize its social value and support appropriate access","Limit access because electronic relationships are necessarily superficial","Treat it chiefly as entertainment with little care relevance","Replace family contact with general health videos"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Electronic contact can sustain important relationships and reduce isolation, including for people with limited mobility.","source":"HCA200 Chapter 23 Quiz Preparation Notes · §2","originalId":"c23_004","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_005","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"Electronic communication","prompt":"A group message reaches ten care staff, but only four need the client's details. Which conclusion is strongest?","choices":["Efficient delivery does not establish that every recipient is authorized","Membership in a care group establishes a need to know","Confidentiality depends primarily on the group's size","A work-related purpose makes recipient selection unnecessary"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Ease of distribution must be separated from authorization and need to know.","source":"HCA200 Chapter 23 Quiz Preparation Notes · §2","originalId":"c23_005","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_006","chapter":23,"mode":"level1","difficulty":1,"topic":"Electronic communication","prompt":"Which is a potential benefit of social media in healthcare?","choices":["Greater participation in managing one's health","Removal of the need for source checking","Elimination of therapeutic boundaries","Equal access regardless of equipment or skills"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Information, support and participation may empower people; none of the other outcomes follows automatically.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 3, 13","originalId":"c23_006","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_007","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Electronic communication","prompt":"Which statement best describes online health information?","choices":["It can contain both reliable information and misinformation","It is reliable when shared by several friends","It is accurate when written in professional language","It becomes evidence when it attracts enough agreement"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Appearance and popularity do not establish accuracy. Both reliable and unreliable information circulate online.","source":"HCA200 Chapter 23 Quiz Preparation Notes · §7","originalId":"c23_007","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_008","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Electronic communication","prompt":"A resident has internet access but cannot navigate an app. Which barrier remains?","choices":["Digital skills","Geographic distance","Lack of any connection","Excessive source credibility"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Connectivity does not supply the skills needed to use an interface.","source":"Chapter 23 · Professional communication using social media","originalId":"c23_008","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_009","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"Electronic communication","prompt":"A service offers online support as its only communication route. Which concern is most justified?","choices":["Some people lack skills or prefer not to disclose information online","Everyone will receive more personalized care","Online availability removes communication barriers","Reluctance to participate necessarily indicates poor motivation"],"answer":[0],"explain":"People may have privacy concerns, limited skills or different communication preferences.","source":"Chapter 23 · Professional communication using social media","originalId":"c23_009","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_010","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Electronic communication","prompt":"Which claim about social media's health benefits is most defensible?","choices":["It can support positive outcomes, but benefits are not guaranteed","It consistently improves every therapeutic relationship","It reduces costs whenever it replaces personal contact","It makes further evaluation unnecessary"],"answer":[0],"explain":"The material describes potential benefits and some positive evidence while recognizing challenges and the need for research.","source":"Chapter 23 · Professional communication using social media","originalId":"c23_010","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_011","chapter":23,"mode":"level1","difficulty":1,"topic":"Absent receivers","prompt":"Which is a nonverbal cue?","choices":["A facial expression","A written diagnosis","A spoken instruction","A typed question"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Facial expressions, gestures and body language communicate without words.","source":"HCA200 Chapter 23 Quiz Preparation Notes · §3","originalId":"c23_011","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_012","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Absent receivers","prompt":"Which cue can a telephone call preserve when ordinary text cannot?","choices":["Changes in vocal tone","Facial expressions","Body posture","Physical gestures"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Voice calls carry vocal cues but do not provide the visual information of a video or in-person encounter.","source":"HCA200 Chapter 23 Quiz Preparation Notes · §3","originalId":"c23_012","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_013","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Absent receivers","prompt":"A brief text meant as reassurance sounds dismissive to its reader. What best explains this?","choices":["Intended meaning and interpreted meaning can differ without contextual cues","Written reassurance is inherently disrespectful","The reader must have ignored the literal words","Typing prevents emotional meaning"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Missing cues and different contexts can change how a message is understood.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 5; notes §3","originalId":"c23_013","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_014","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"Absent receivers","prompt":"A worker says, “Nobody complained immediately, so the post caused no harm.” Which assumption makes that conclusion unreliable?","choices":["Assuming an absent audience’s silence is reliable evidence of no harm","Readers can interpret the same words differently","Posts can be redistributed","The sender has an intended meaning"],"answer":[0],"explain":"In one-way communication, lack of an immediate response does not establish the audience's reaction.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 5","originalId":"c23_014","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_015","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Absent receivers","prompt":"Which statement best describes the risk of misunderstanding an electronic message?","choices":["A message can be misunderstood despite a considerate intention.","A considerate intention establishes that any misunderstanding is the reader’s error.","A delayed reply establishes that the reader disagrees with the message.","A delivery receipt establishes that the intended meaning was understood."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Intent, delivery, understanding and agreement are distinct. Appropriate wording and confirmation can reduce ambiguity.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 5, 10","originalId":"c23_015","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_016","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"Absent receivers","prompt":"A student posts 'I hate this placement' when struggling with its pace. Readers avoid the site. What is the central communication failure?","choices":["A personal reaction was presented without context and interpreted as a judgment of the site","Readers received too much clinical information","The placement's quality was independently established","The student's private intention determined the public meaning"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Readers cannot access an unstated distinction between struggling with pace and condemning a placement.","source":"Chapter 23 · Other factors relating to the use of social media","originalId":"c23_016","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_017","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Absent receivers","prompt":"Why can composing a post feel more controllable than its consequences actually are?","choices":["The sender controls the initial wording but not all interpretations or redistribution","The sender can determine who will remember it","The sender receives every reader's reaction","The platform prevents messages from leaving the original group"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Control of composition does not mean control of the audience or subsequent use.","source":"Chapter 23 · Social networking sites","originalId":"c23_017","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_018","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Absent receivers","prompt":"Which response best reduces ambiguity in an important electronic message?","choices":["Use clear wording and seek confirmation of meaning when appropriate","Assume familiar readers will supply the missing context","Rely on punctuation to replace every nonverbal cue","Treat a delivery receipt as proof of agreement"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Clear wording and feedback help address the loss of context. Delivery does not establish understanding.","source":"HCA200 Chapter 23 Quiz Preparation Notes · §3","originalId":"c23_018","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_019","chapter":23,"mode":"level1","difficulty":1,"topic":"Absent receivers","prompt":"A team replaces an in-person discussion with a video call. Which limitation should it still consider?","choices":["The call may convey some nonverbal cues without reproducing the full in-person context.","Visible faces guarantee that every gesture and reaction will be understood.","A video connection removes the need to check for misunderstanding.","The availability of sound establishes that every participant can participate equally."],"answer":[0],"explain":"A video call preserves more cues than text but does not reproduce every feature of an in-person exchange or guarantee shared understanding.","source":"HCA200 Chapter 23 Quiz Preparation Notes · §3","originalId":"c23_019","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_020","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"Absent receivers","prompt":"A joke embarrasses a colleague in a large course forum. Which response best shows accountability?","choices":["Consider the audience's experience and address the impact rather than relying on the joke's intention","Explain that humour makes professional expectations irrelevant","Ask only whether close friends found it funny","Assume embarrassment ends when the thread stops"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Electronic jokes can affect the target and observers, and can communicate attitudes about the sender.","source":"Chapter 23 · Social networking sites","originalId":"c23_020","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_021","chapter":23,"mode":"level1","difficulty":1,"topic":"Cyberbullying","prompt":"Which feature most directly makes bullying cyberbullying?","choices":["Use of electronic communication","An unknown perpetrator","An audience of strangers","A public profile"],"answer":[0],"explain":"The electronic medium is the defining cyber element; anonymity and publicity are not required.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 7–8","originalId":"c23_021","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_022","chapter":23,"mode":"level1","difficulty":1,"topic":"Cyberbullying","prompt":"Who is the perpetrator?","choices":["The person or group carrying out the bullying","The person targeted","The person who reports observing it","The supervisor receiving a report"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Roles depend on conduct: perpetrator, victim and observer have different meanings.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 9","originalId":"c23_022","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_023","chapter":23,"mode":"level1","difficulty":1,"topic":"Cyberbullying","prompt":"Who is the observer?","choices":["Someone who witnesses or becomes aware of bullying","Only the person formally investigating it","Only someone physically beside the victim","Someone who must have joined the aggression"],"answer":[0],"explain":"An observer can become aware of electronic bullying without being physically present or participating.","source":"HCA200 Chapter 23 Quiz Preparation Notes · §4","originalId":"c23_023","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_024","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Cyberbullying","prompt":"Which pattern best fits bullying?","choices":["Repeated intentional aggression toward someone typically unable to defend themselves","One respectful disagreement about a care plan","Constructive feedback with specific examples","A mutual debate without an intention to harm"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Intentional harm, repetition and vulnerability distinguish the described bullying pattern from disagreement or feedback.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 7","originalId":"c23_024","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_025","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Cyberbullying","prompt":"Repeated messages use sexual innuendo to humiliate a colleague. The sender calls them jokes. What matters most?","choices":["The pattern of aggression and harmful intent, not simply the label 'joke'","Whether the messages contain a direct threat","Whether the colleague responds publicly","Whether the sender uses a real name"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Cyberbullying can involve sexual innuendo and ridicule; it need not include an explicit threat.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 8","originalId":"c23_025","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_026","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"Cyberbullying","prompt":"A supervisor privately gives evidence-based corrective feedback through an approved system. Why is this not enough, by itself, to establish cyberbullying?","choices":["Electronic criticism alone does not establish deliberate aggression intended to harm","Only anonymous people can cyberbully","Private messages cannot be bullying","Supervisors cannot be perpetrators"],"answer":[0],"explain":"The medium alone is insufficient; context and the nature of the conduct matter.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 7–8","originalId":"c23_026","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_027","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Cyberbullying","prompt":"How can pseudonyms contribute to cyberbullying?","choices":["They may make perpetrators harder to identify","They remove the possibility of harm","They make every message bullying","They establish that the sender cannot be traced"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Apparent anonymity can encourage continued aggression, but it does not guarantee untraceability.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 8","originalId":"c23_027","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_028","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Cyberbullying","prompt":"A coworker repeatedly posts lies meant to undermine another worker's competence. Which form of harm is illustrated?","choices":["An attack on professional abilities","Ordinary professional development","Authorized performance appraisal","Neutral information sharing"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Lies and attacks designed to undermine professional abilities are recognized forms of cyberbullying.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 8; notes §4","originalId":"c23_028","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_029","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Cyberbullying","prompt":"What should an observer of workplace bullying do?","choices":["Support the targeted person and use appropriate workplace reporting channels","Wait for the victim to confront the group alone","Redistribute the messages to warn everyone","Stay silent unless personally targeted"],"answer":[0],"explain":"The study notes emphasize support and appropriate reporting rather than silence or wider circulation.","source":"HCA200 Chapter 23 Quiz Preparation Notes · §4","originalId":"c23_029","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_030","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"Cyberbullying","prompt":"A victim asks a witness for support but fears reporting. Which response is most appropriate?","choices":["Listen respectfully, explain available reporting routes and help them access support","Promise absolute secrecy regardless of workplace responsibilities","Post the story publicly to force action","Treat reluctance to report as proof the harm is minor"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Fear, shame and helplessness can inhibit reporting. Support should help the person use appropriate channels.","source":"HCA200 Chapter 23 Quiz Preparation Notes · §4; Chapter 23 · Cyberbullying","originalId":"c23_030","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_031","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Cyberbullying","prompt":"Why might cyberbullying go unreported?","choices":["Shame and concern about others' reactions","Lack of any psychological effect","Automatic resolution after blocking","Certainty that observers will report it"],"answer":[0],"explain":"People may feel ashamed, helpless or reluctant to depend on others, even when harm is serious.","source":"Chapter 23 · Cyberbullying","originalId":"c23_031","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_032","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"Cyberbullying","prompt":"A target blocks the aggressor. Which conclusion follows most reasonably?","choices":["Access may be reduced while psychological harm can persist","The bullying's effects have necessarily ended","Reporting is now inappropriate","Observers can no longer be affected"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Ending access does not automatically undo psychological damage to a victim or observer.","source":"Chapter 23 · Cyberbullying","originalId":"c23_032","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_033","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Cyberbullying","prompt":"Which statement about observers is accurate?","choices":["Witnessing cyberbullying can also affect their well-being","Observers are unaffected unless they reply","Observers become perpetrators simply by seeing a message","Observers have no role in workplace responses"],"answer":[0],"explain":"The material recognizes possible harm to observers as well as victims, alongside the importance of responding appropriately.","source":"Chapter 23 · Cyberbullying; notes §4","originalId":"c23_033","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_034","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"Cyberbullying","prompt":"Which claim is NOT supported?","choices":["Cyberbullying requires the perpetrator to hide their identity","Electronic aggression can include threats","Victims may hesitate to disclose harm","Repeated ridicule can damage well-being"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Anonymity may contribute, but known individuals can also engage in cyberbullying.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 8–9","originalId":"c23_034","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_035","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Cyberbullying","prompt":"Which consequence can occur in severe cyberbullying situations?","choices":["Serious psychological distress and suicidal thoughts","Only temporary irritation","Guaranteed immediate recovery after deletion","Harm limited to employment prospects"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Consequences can be serious, including major mental health difficulties; severity should not be minimized.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 9","originalId":"c23_035","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_036","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"Cyberbullying","prompt":"A worker sees repeated humiliating posts but says, 'I did not write them, so ignoring them is the best professional response.' What is the error?","choices":["Confusing not perpetrating harm with responding appropriately as an observer","Confusing an observer with the person targeted","Assuming every workplace concern is electronic","Assuming reporting is itself an act of aggression"],"answer":[0],"explain":"An observer has a constructive role in support and appropriate reporting even without authoring the messages.","source":"HCA200 Chapter 23 Quiz Preparation Notes · §4","originalId":"c23_036","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_037","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Cyberbullying","prompt":"Which pair correctly matches roles?","choices":["Targeted worker—victim; witness—observer","Targeted worker—observer; witness—perpetrator","Sender of threats—victim; recipient—perpetrator","Reporter—perpetrator; target—observer"],"answer":[0],"explain":"The person targeted is the victim; a witness is an observer. Reporting does not make someone a perpetrator.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 9","originalId":"c23_037","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_038","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"Cyberbullying","prompt":"Which statement avoids both minimizing harm and overlabelling disagreement?","choices":["Assess deliberate harmful aggression and its pattern in context","Classify every unwelcome online opinion as cyberbullying","Dismiss aggression unless it includes physical violence","Classify harm only after a public complaint"],"answer":[0],"explain":"The definitions focus on deliberate harmful aggression, with repetition explicit in the reading. Electronic aggression need not be physical.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 7–8; Chapter 23 · Cyberbullying","originalId":"c23_038","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_039","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Cyberbullying","prompt":"A named colleague sends repeated threatening texts directly to a worker. Which fact would NOT rule out cyberbullying?","choices":["The sender's identity is known","The communication is entirely non-electronic","The interaction lacks deliberate aggression","The messages are respectful and intended to help"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Known identity does not prevent cyberbullying. The other options remove central features of the described conduct.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 8","originalId":"c23_039","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_040","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"Cyberbullying","prompt":"A worker wants to support a target without amplifying the humiliation. Which choice best serves that goal?","choices":["Use appropriate confidential workplace channels rather than forwarding the post broadly","Tag the entire team so every person can judge the target","Copy the insults into a public defence of the target","Invite classmates outside work to identify the perpetrator"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Support and reporting should avoid unnecessarily extending the harmful audience.","source":"HCA200 Chapter 23 Quiz Preparation Notes · §4","originalId":"c23_040","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_041","chapter":23,"mode":"level1","difficulty":1,"topic":"Privacy & permanence","prompt":"A worker deletes a post after realizing it was inappropriate. Which conclusion about its availability is most defensible?","choices":["Copies, screenshots or forwarded versions may remain.","Deleting the original removes every recipient’s copy.","A post can persist only if it originally used a public account.","A deleted post has no possible professional consequences."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Deletion can remove the original without removing copies or consequences. Consider potential retention and circulation before posting.","source":"HCA200 Chapter 23 Quiz Preparation Notes · §5","originalId":"c23_041","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_042","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Privacy & permanence","prompt":"Which assumption is least safe before posting in a closed group?","choices":["Only the intended audience will ever see the content","Members may interpret the content differently","Someone may copy a message","Posts can affect professional impressions"],"answer":[0],"explain":"A restricted audience does not ensure that material stays within that audience.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 10; notes §5","originalId":"c23_042","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_043","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Privacy & permanence","prompt":"A staff selfie contains a resident and a care memo in the background. What is the main issue?","choices":["Unintended disclosure can occur through background details","Only the intended subject of a photo matters","A casual photo cannot communicate health information","Removing the caption eliminates disclosure"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Images may reveal people, documents and contextual identifiers even when the caption names nobody.","source":"HCA200 Chapter 23 Quiz Preparation Notes · §2","originalId":"c23_043","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_044","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"Privacy & permanence","prompt":"A worker removes a client's name but leaves a rare diagnosis, room number and admission date. What is the strongest concern?","choices":["The remaining details may still identify the client","Name removal always establishes anonymity","A diagnosis is identifying only when a photo is included","Dates matter only in public accounts"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Confidentiality requires attention to all identifying details, not just names.","source":"Chapter 23 · Professional communication using social media","originalId":"c23_044","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_045","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Privacy & permanence","prompt":"A personal post says nothing about work but publicly ridicules vulnerable people. Why can it still matter professionally?","choices":["Personal and professional identities influence each other","Professional identity exists only during paid shifts","Public attitudes are unrelated to trust","Only posts naming an employer affect credibility"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Readers can infer attitudes and trustworthiness from personal posts, affecting professional impressions.","source":"Chapter 23 · Introduction; Netiquette","originalId":"c23_045","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_046","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"Privacy & permanence","prompt":"A worker writes 'Views are my own' beneath an insulting post. Which conclusion is best?","choices":["The disclaimer does not remove the post's possible professional consequences","The disclaimer separates personal identity completely from professional identity","Only the disclaimer should guide an employer's judgment","The disclaimer prevents readers from sharing the post"],"answer":[0],"explain":"A disclaimer does not control interpretation, reputation or circulation.","source":"Chapter 23 · Introduction; Other factors relating to the use of social media","originalId":"c23_046","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_047","chapter":23,"mode":"level1","difficulty":1,"topic":"Privacy & permanence","prompt":"Which three considerations apply to both personal and professional social media use?","choices":["Privacy, security and legality","Popularity, speed and entertainment","Anonymity, humour and reach","Frequency, brevity and convenience"],"answer":[0],"explain":"The material repeatedly emphasizes privacy, security and legality.","source":"HCA200 Chapter 23 Quiz Preparation Notes · §5","originalId":"c23_047","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_048","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Privacy & permanence","prompt":"What may a prospective employer infer from public social media activity?","choices":["Professionalism and credibility","Guaranteed clinical competence","A complete employment history","The applicant's consent to disclose client records"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Public activity can inform impressions of employability but does not establish a full record of competence.","source":"HCA200 Chapter 23 Quiz Preparation Notes · §5","originalId":"c23_048","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_049","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"Privacy & permanence","prompt":"A worker says, 'It happened outside work, so my public post cannot affect community trust in me as a caregiver.' Which distinction is missed?","choices":["The setting of posting differs from the reach of its professional effects","Only work devices create public content","A post is professional only when paid for","Community trust depends solely on qualifications"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Personal activity can affect professional identity and public trust.","source":"Chapter 23 · Other factors relating to the use of social media","originalId":"c23_049","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_050","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Privacy & permanence","prompt":"Which is the best route for discussing frustration about a placement's time pressures?","choices":["An appropriate conversation with the supervisor","A vague public complaint about the site","A personal post naming no staff but describing the shift","A closed social group with classmates and no supervisor"],"answer":[0],"explain":"The placement example illustrates how personal workplace comments can be misunderstood. Appropriate workplace discussion offers context and feedback.","source":"Chapter 23 · Other factors relating to the use of social media","originalId":"c23_050","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_051","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Privacy & permanence","prompt":"A post is accurate but reveals confidential client details. Which judgment is correct?","choices":["Truth alone does not make disclosure appropriate","Accuracy establishes permission","Confidentiality matters only if the claim is false","A true post meets every NETIQUETTE criterion"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Accuracy and authorization are different requirements. A true statement can still violate privacy.","source":"HCA200 Chapter 23 Quiz Preparation Notes · §5–6","originalId":"c23_051","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_052","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"Privacy & permanence","prompt":"A resident agrees to a photo. What else must the worker consider before sharing it through work?","choices":["Employer policy, the scope of consent and any other confidential details","Whether agreement automatically permits every platform","Whether the picture is flattering enough to override policy","Whether staff approval can replace the resident's consent"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Consent has a scope and does not erase policy requirements or other people's privacy interests.","source":"HCA200 Chapter 23 Quiz Preparation Notes · §2, §5","originalId":"c23_052","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_053","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Privacy & permanence","prompt":"Why is an email not automatically temporary or consequence-free?","choices":["It can be retained or forwarded and may have professional implications","Only social networking posts are retained","A private recipient cannot copy it","Emails disappear once read"],"answer":[0],"explain":"The permanence and circulation concerns apply to email as well as social networking comments.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 10","originalId":"c23_053","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_054","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"Privacy & permanence","prompt":"A message was intended for three friends but reaches a future employer. Which lesson follows?","choices":["Compose with potential wider circulation in mind","Assume friendship establishes confidentiality","Treat the original audience as the only relevant audience","Judge risk solely by the number of initial recipients"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Initial audience size does not determine eventual reach.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 10–11","originalId":"c23_054","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_055","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Privacy & permanence","prompt":"A worker uses a personal account to communicate with a client. Which principle still applies?","choices":["Professional boundaries and applicable employer guidance remain relevant.","A personal account removes professional duties when the worker is off shift.","A private group authorizes discussion of any client detail.","Deleting the exchange later removes the need for a boundary check."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Personal accounts and private groups do not eliminate confidentiality, role limits or professional boundaries.","source":"HCA200 Chapter 23 Quiz Preparation Notes · §5, §7","originalId":"c23_055","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_056","chapter":23,"mode":"level1","difficulty":1,"topic":"Privacy & permanence","prompt":"For the simplified study distinction, written defamation is generally called what?","choices":["Libel","Slander","Netiquette","Empathy"],"answer":[0],"explain":"The notes distinguish written or recorded defamation (libel) from spoken defamation (slander). This is a study distinction, not a full legal test.","source":"HCA200 Chapter 23 Quiz Preparation Notes · §5","originalId":"c23_056","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_057","chapter":23,"mode":"level1","difficulty":1,"topic":"Privacy & permanence","prompt":"For the simplified study distinction, spoken defamation is generally called what?","choices":["Slander","Libel","Disclosure consent","Cybersecurity"],"answer":[0],"explain":"The notes use slander for spoken defamation. The distinction does not settle the legal status of a particular case.","source":"HCA200 Chapter 23 Quiz Preparation Notes · §5","originalId":"c23_057","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_058","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"Privacy & permanence","prompt":"A quiz scenario contrasts a false written allegation online with a false spoken allegation. Which pairing matches the simplified terminology?","choices":["Written—libel; spoken—slander","Written—slander; spoken—libel","Written—privacy; spoken—security","Written—slander; spoken—confidentiality"],"answer":[0],"explain":"The simplified distinction concerns the form of defamation. Whether a particular allegation meets a legal test requires more information.","source":"HCA200 Chapter 23 Quiz Preparation Notes · §5","originalId":"c23_058","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_059","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Privacy & permanence","prompt":"Why is 'I removed the person's name' an incomplete privacy check?","choices":["Images and contextual details may still reveal identity","Names are never identifying","Only employers can recognize a client","Privacy depends entirely on whether the person complains"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Identifiability can arise from combinations of details or images.","source":"Chapter 23 · Professional communication using social media","originalId":"c23_059","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_060","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"Privacy & permanence","prompt":"Which proposed rule most effectively addresses both audience and permanence?","choices":["Share only appropriate content after considering who may eventually see or retain it","Post freely if the account is private and delete later","Use disappearing messages instead of checking authorization","Assume trusted recipients will prevent further copies"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Appropriateness must be evaluated before posting because later control over reach and retention is limited.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 10–11; notes §5","originalId":"c23_060","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_061","chapter":23,"mode":"level1","difficulty":1,"topic":"NETIQUETTE","prompt":"A post is accurate and respectful but adds nothing needed in the current discussion. Which NETIQUETTE criterion most directly asks whether it belongs there?","choices":["Necessary","True","Empathic","Trustworthy"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Necessary asks what makes a comment needed. Accuracy and respectful tone address other criteria and do not establish necessity.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 12","originalId":"c23_061","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_062","chapter":23,"mode":"level1","difficulty":1,"topic":"NETIQUETTE","prompt":"A student asks whether a proposed comment is worthy of respect, rather than merely accurate or well timed. Which course NETIQUETTE term addresses that question?","choices":["Estimable","True","Timely","Efficacy"],"answer":[0],"explain":"In the supplied mnemonic, Estimable concerns being worthy of respect. Do not substitute one positive criterion for every other check.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 12","originalId":"c23_062","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_063","chapter":23,"mode":"level1","difficulty":1,"topic":"NETIQUETTE","prompt":"A health post is encouraging but its factual claim has not been checked. Which NETIQUETTE term most directly requires evidence for accuracy?","choices":["True","Inspiring","Quotable","Useful"],"answer":[0],"explain":"True concerns accuracy. A claim may sound inspiring or useful while still lacking evidence.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 12","originalId":"c23_063","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_064","chapter":23,"mode":"level1","difficulty":1,"topic":"NETIQUETTE","prompt":"A comment is accurate and relevant, and the writer also wants it to encourage hope. Which NETIQUETTE criterion most directly addresses that additional aim?","choices":["Inspiring","True","Necessary","Trustworthy"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Inspiring concerns encouragement or a positive effect on the audience; it does not replace checking accuracy and other criteria.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 12","originalId":"c23_064","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_065","chapter":23,"mode":"level1","difficulty":1,"topic":"NETIQUETTE","prompt":"A writer asks whether others would be proud to repeat a comment, rather than simply whether it is easy to copy. Which NETIQUETTE criterion is being applied?","choices":["Quotable","Useful","Timely","Efficacy"],"answer":[0],"explain":"The supplied mnemonic uses Quotable to ask whether others would be proud to repeat the comment, not merely whether it can spread.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 12","originalId":"c23_065","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_066","chapter":23,"mode":"level1","difficulty":1,"topic":"NETIQUETTE","prompt":"A post is respectful and accurate but offers no relevant help to its intended readers. Which NETIQUETTE criterion most directly addresses that gap?","choices":["Useful","Estimable","True","Empathic"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Useful considers the value to the audience. Respect and accuracy do not by themselves establish usefulness.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 12","originalId":"c23_066","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_067","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"NETIQUETTE","prompt":"Which sequence correctly completes NETIQUETTE after Useful?","choices":["Efficacy, Trustworthy, Timely, Empathic","Empathic, True, Timely, Estimable","Estimable, Timely, Trustworthy, Efficacy","Efficacy, Timely, True, Empathic"],"answer":[0],"explain":"The final four terms are Efficacy, Trustworthy, Timely and Empathic.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 12","originalId":"c23_067","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_068","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"NETIQUETTE","prompt":"Which term most directly asks, 'What consequences could this have for me and others?'","choices":["Efficacy","Estimable","Quotable","Necessary"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Here Efficacy concerns the effects and consequences of a comment.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 12; Chapter 23 · Netiquette","originalId":"c23_068","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_069","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"NETIQUETTE","prompt":"Which term most directly asks whether the comment shows consideration for another person's feelings?","choices":["Empathic","Efficacy","Estimable","True"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Empathic focuses on understanding and consideration of others' feelings.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 12; Chapter 23 · Netiquette","originalId":"c23_069","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_070","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"NETIQUETTE","prompt":"Which term most directly asks whether a comment demonstrates that its author deserves confidence?","choices":["Trustworthy","True","Timely","Quotable"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Trustworthy concerns being worthy of trust; True focuses more specifically on evidence and accuracy.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 12; Chapter 23 · Netiquette","originalId":"c23_070","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_071","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"NETIQUETTE","prompt":"A message is accurate but interrupts a sensitive discussion at an inappropriate moment. Which criterion most directly addresses the moment and context?","choices":["Timely","True","Quotable","Estimable"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Timely asks whether a comment is appropriate in relation to the situation and other comments.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 12; Chapter 23 · Netiquette","originalId":"c23_071","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_072","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"NETIQUETTE","prompt":"A post is popular, but nobody can provide evidence for its claim. Which check is most directly unmet?","choices":["True","Quotable","Timely","Inspiring"],"answer":[0],"explain":"True requires evidence. Popularity does not verify a claim.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 12; Chapter 23 · Netiquette","originalId":"c23_072","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_073","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"NETIQUETTE","prompt":"A worker says, 'It will be repeated by hundreds, so it is Quotable.' What is missing?","choices":["Whether others would be proud to repeat it","Whether it is short enough to copy","Whether the author can disable comments","Whether it uses a memorable slogan"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Quotable is an ethical quality check, not a prediction of viral circulation.","source":"Chapter 23 · Netiquette","originalId":"c23_073","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_074","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"NETIQUETTE","prompt":"A respectful post provides no relevant help to its audience. Which distinction is most directly tested?","choices":["Estimable does not necessarily mean Useful","Useful and True mean the same thing","Timely always implies Necessary","Empathic automatically implies Quotable"],"answer":[0],"explain":"A message can be admirable yet offer little value to a particular audience. Each criterion asks a different question.","source":"Chapter 23 · Netiquette","originalId":"c23_074","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_075","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"NETIQUETTE","prompt":"Which pair is correctly matched?","choices":["Estimable—worthy of respect; Efficacy—consequences","Estimable—speed; Efficacy—accuracy","Estimable—empathy; Efficacy—popularity","Estimable—necessity; Efficacy—anonymity"],"answer":[0],"explain":"The first E concerns esteem; the later E concerns effects.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 12; Chapter 23 · Netiquette","originalId":"c23_075","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_076","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"NETIQUETTE","prompt":"A message encourages hope but uses an unsupported claim. Which judgment is strongest?","choices":["It may be Inspiring while failing the True check","Being Inspiring establishes its accuracy","Being positive makes evidence unnecessary","It fails only the Timely check"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Positive tone cannot substitute for accurate, supported content.","source":"Chapter 23 · Netiquette","originalId":"c23_076","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_077","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"NETIQUETTE","prompt":"Which question most directly tests Necessary?","choices":["What makes this comment needed?","Would others be proud to repeat it?","Does it demonstrate empathy?","What evidence supports it?"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Necessary concerns whether there is a reason to make the comment; the other questions test different terms.","source":"Chapter 23 · Netiquette","originalId":"c23_077","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_078","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"NETIQUETTE","prompt":"Which sequence lists all ten terms in order?","choices":["Necessary, Estimable, True, Inspiring, Quotable, Useful, Efficacy, Trustworthy, Timely, Empathic","Necessary, Empathic, True, Inspiring, Quotable, Useful, Efficacy, Timely, Trustworthy, Estimable","Necessary, Estimable, Trustworthy, Inspiring, Quotable, Useful, Efficacy, True, Timely, Empathic","Necessary, Estimable, True, Inspiring, Quotable, Useful, Empathic, Trustworthy, Timely, Efficacy"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Pay particular attention to the three E terms and three T terms: their positions are not interchangeable.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 12","originalId":"c23_078","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_079","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"NETIQUETTE","prompt":"Which is one of the recommended checks before posting?","choices":["Would I say this to the person face-to-face?","Can I remove it before anyone saves it?","Will enough people agree to make it acceptable?","Can I keep my professional identity entirely separate?"],"answer":[0],"explain":"The face-to-face check encourages accountability for electronic communication.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 11","originalId":"c23_079","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_080","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"NETIQUETTE","prompt":"A worker would never say a humiliating comment in person but feels comfortable typing it. What should that difference prompt?","choices":["Reflection on why electronic distance seems to lower their standards","Confidence that typing changes its ethical meaning","Assurance that absent receivers cannot be hurt","A decision based only on whether the comment is true"],"answer":[0],"explain":"The pre-posting questions ask users to apply interpersonal consideration even when the receiver is absent.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 11; Chapter 23 · Social networking sites","originalId":"c23_080","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_081","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"NETIQUETTE","prompt":"A writer asks whether wording shows consideration for another person’s feelings. Which NETIQUETTE criterion is most directly being tested?","choices":["Empathic","Efficacy","Estimable","True"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Empathic concerns consideration of others’ feelings. The mnemonic’s criteria are related but ask distinct questions.","source":"HCA200 Chapter 23 Quiz Preparation Notes · §6; slides 12","originalId":"c23_081","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_082","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"NETIQUETTE","prompt":"Which statement best describes how to use the mnemonic?","choices":["Evaluate the message across distinct criteria rather than treating one good quality as permission to post","Post whenever at least one term applies","Use it only for public professional accounts","Use it after posting to decide whether deletion is necessary"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Accuracy, usefulness, effects, trust and empathy address different aspects of responsible communication before posting.","source":"Chapter 23 · Netiquette","originalId":"c23_082","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_083","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"NETIQUETTE","prompt":"Which pair correctly distinguishes True from Trustworthy?","choices":["Evidence for accuracy versus demonstrating that the sender deserves trust","Positive tone versus a convenient posting time","A necessary comment versus one worth repeating","Empathy for the target versus usefulness for the audience"],"answer":[0],"explain":"The two T terms are related but focus on different questions: support for a claim and the trust communicated by conduct.","source":"Chapter 23 · Netiquette","originalId":"c23_083","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_084","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"NETIQUETTE","prompt":"An announcement is useful and accurate, but its wording mocks people who need help. Which criterion most directly exposes that problem?","choices":["Empathic","Timely","True","Necessary"],"answer":[0],"explain":"A message's practical value and accuracy do not excuse disregard for the feelings and dignity of its audience.","source":"Chapter 23 · Netiquette","originalId":"c23_084","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_085","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Professional use","prompt":"Which is a constructive professional use of social media?","choices":["Sharing approved, credible health education with the intended audience","Diagnosing acquaintances through a personal comment thread","Discussing identifiable client stories in a casual group","Accepting client friend requests to avoid all discomfort"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Professional use can promote education and support while respecting confidentiality and boundaries.","source":"HCA200 Day 5 Social Media · slides 13; notes §7","originalId":"c23_085","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_086","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"Professional use","prompt":"A client sends a personal friend request. What is the best response?","choices":["Follow professional and employer guidance and explain an appropriate communication route respectfully","Accept because declining could feel impolite","Ignore all future care communication from the client","Accept but ask the client never to mention treatment"],"answer":[0],"explain":"A respectful explanation and appropriate channel help preserve the therapeutic relationship and its boundaries.","source":"Chapter 23 · Professional communication using social media","originalId":"c23_086","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_087","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Professional use","prompt":"Why might separate personal and professional accounts be helpful?","choices":["They can support clearer purposes and boundaries","They guarantee no personal post can affect professional reputation","They authorize client disclosure on the professional account","They remove the need to follow employer policy"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Separate accounts can help manage boundaries, but they do not remove responsibilities or reputational connections.","source":"Chapter 23 · Professional communication using social media","originalId":"c23_087","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_088","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Professional use","prompt":"A client declines an online support group because they do not want to be known as a patient. What should be recognized?","choices":["Privacy and identity concerns can be legitimate reasons to decline","Refusal shows that the client does not want support","The group must therefore be clinically unreliable","Participation should be required to demonstrate engagement"],"answer":[0],"explain":"People may avoid sites because they do not want to disclose their patient identity or personal information.","source":"Chapter 23 · Professional communication using social media","originalId":"c23_088","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_089","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"Professional use","prompt":"A health claim is shared widely in a support group. What is the best next step before using it in education?","choices":["Check credible evidence, relevance and source quality","Count supportive comments","Use it if a healthcare worker reposted it","Prefer whichever version sounds most confident"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Source verification is necessary; professional appearance and repeated sharing are not substitutes for evidence.","source":"HCA200 Chapter 23 Quiz Preparation Notes · §7","originalId":"c23_089","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_090","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Professional use","prompt":"Which choice best supports a diverse online audience?","choices":["Adapt language and communication style to the audience","Use the same technical wording for every reader","Assume access means understanding","Prioritize the sender's preferred jargon"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Professional communication should accommodate audience needs rather than assume uniform skills or understanding.","source":"Chapter 23 · Professional communication using social media","originalId":"c23_090","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_091","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Professional use","prompt":"A webinar lets colleagues discuss recent research without travelling. Which benefits are illustrated?","choices":["Professional learning and communication across distance","Guaranteed agreement and universal access","Elimination of source checking and confidentiality duties","Automatic improvement in every participant's care"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Electronic professional learning can share research and advice across distance, while its limitations remain.","source":"Chapter 23 · Professional communication using social media","originalId":"c23_091","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_092","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"Professional use","prompt":"A support group offers practical peer experiences. Which interpretation is most balanced?","choices":["Shared experience can support participation, while health claims still need evaluation","Lived experience makes all advice clinically reliable","Peer support has no role unless a professional writes every post","A supportive tone removes privacy concerns"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Peer support can be empowering without making every medical claim reliable or every disclosure appropriate.","source":"Chapter 23 · Professional communication using social media","originalId":"c23_092","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_093","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Professional use","prompt":"Which is a potential disadvantage of excessive social media use?","choices":["Reduced concentration and disrupted sleep","Guaranteed improvement in attention","Automatic strengthening of every relationship","Removal of social anxiety"],"answer":[0],"explain":"The reading associates overuse with concentration problems, mood changes and sleep difficulties; effects vary.","source":"Chapter 23 · Social networking sites","originalId":"c23_093","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_094","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"Professional use","prompt":"A resident gains companionship online but begins missing sleep to remain connected. What is the most balanced approach?","choices":["Support the valuable connection while addressing the harmful pattern of overuse","Remove access because online contact has no social value","Ignore sleep because social benefits cancel all risks","Assume more online time always improves well-being"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Benefits and harms can coexist. Support should recognize meaningful connection and address overuse.","source":"Chapter 23 · Social networking sites; notes §2","originalId":"c23_094","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_095","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Professional use","prompt":"Which statement about comment threads is most accurate?","choices":["Existing positive or negative comments can influence later responses","Each response is independent of the thread's tone","Negative comments necessarily improve critical thinking","Positive comments establish the truth of a claim"],"answer":[0],"explain":"The reading describes how the tone of existing comments can encourage similar responses; agreement does not prove accuracy.","source":"Chapter 23 · Social networking sites","originalId":"c23_095","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_096","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"Professional use","prompt":"A worker shares a credible research summary through an approved channel but includes an identifiable client example unnecessarily. Which judgment is best?","choices":["The educational value does not excuse the confidentiality problem","Credibility makes the entire message appropriate","Approval of the channel authorizes every disclosure","Professional recipients remove all privacy concerns"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Content and authorization matter as well as channel and source quality.","source":"Chapter 23 · Professional communication using social media","originalId":"c23_096","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_097","chapter":23,"mode":"level2","difficulty":2,"topic":"Professional use","prompt":"A worker maintains a separate professional account and receives a client’s request for personal contact. Which conclusion is most appropriate?","choices":["Separate accounts can help with boundaries but do not remove the need to follow professional guidance.","A professional account authorizes any request initiated by the client.","Separate accounts prevent personal content from affecting professional trust.","The account label alone determines whether an exchange is confidential."],"answer":[0],"explain":"Account separation can clarify purposes, but it does not eliminate boundaries, confidentiality obligations or employer requirements.","source":"HCA200 Chapter 23 Quiz Preparation Notes · §7; Chapter 23 · Professional communication using social media","originalId":"c23_097","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_098","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"Professional use","prompt":"Which policy approach best addresses professional social media use?","choices":["Combine confidentiality and boundary guidance with applicable employer and professional requirements","Rely only on each worker's good intentions","Apply professional expectations only to paid promotional posts","Use account privacy settings as the complete policy"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Robust guidelines protect clients and professionals and should align with applicable organizational and professional requirements.","source":"Chapter 23 · Professional communication using social media","originalId":"c23_098","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_099","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"Professional use","prompt":"A professional group wants rapid advice about a complex client situation. Which priority best balances usefulness with responsibility?","choices":["Use an appropriate authorized route and limit identifying information to what is permitted and needed","Include the full record so every member has equal access","Use a personal account because it is faster","Assume urgency makes recipient authorization unnecessary"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Speed does not override confidentiality, appropriate recipient selection or professional boundaries.","source":"HCA200 Chapter 23 Quiz Preparation Notes · §2, §5, §7","originalId":"c23_099","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]},{"id":"main/chapter-23/data.js:c23_100","chapter":23,"mode":"level3","difficulty":3,"topic":"Professional use","prompt":"Which conclusion best integrates the advantages and challenges of electronic communication?","choices":["It can connect and empower people when accuracy, privacy, audience and boundaries are actively considered","It is appropriate whenever it is more convenient than a conversation","Its benefits depend mainly on posting frequently","Its risks make every professional use inappropriate"],"answer":[0],"explain":"Responsible use develops the benefits while addressing foreseeable communication and professional challenges.","source":"Chapter 23 · Chapter summary","originalId":"c23_100","group":"Chapter 23","bank":"Chapter 23","sourceFiles":["main/chapter-23/data.js"],"references":[]}]);
